{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggV1zt6CVgux8sK1sK2R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For tetrahedral Co(II), I get **Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**. I worked this out by hand and did not call any of the tools.\n\n**Approach**\n\nA tetrahedral d⁷ ion has the same energy-level pattern as an octahedral d³ ion (the d³ diagram applies with the sign of Dq reversed). The ground state is ⁴A₂(F), so the three spin-allowed bands are assigned as:\n\n- ν₁ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂(F) = 10Dq = 4 070 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F) = 7 030 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P) = 16 400 cm⁻¹\n\nThe two ⁴T₁ levels mix with each other. Their energies are:\n\nE(⁴T₁) = 7.5B + 15Dq ∓ ½·√(225B² + 100Dq² − 180·Dq·B)\n\n**Solving**\n\n1. From ν₁: Dq = 4 070 / 10 = **407 cm⁻¹**\n2. Adding the two ⁴T₁ energies cancels the square root: ν₂ + ν₃ = 15B + 30Dq.\n   - 23 430 = 15B + 12 210\n   - So B = **748 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Consistency check**\n\nMultiplying the two ⁴T₁ energies gives ν₂·ν₃ = 270·B·Dq + 200·Dq².\n\n- Calculated with these values: 1.1533 × 10⁸ cm⁻²\n- Observed (7 030 × 16 400): 1.1529 × 10⁸ cm⁻²\n\nThey agree to within 0.03%, so all three bands fit one set of Dq and B and the assignment holds.\n\n**Notes**\n\n- B = 748 cm⁻¹ is about 77% of the free-ion Co²⁺ value (about 970 cm⁻¹), so β ≈ 0.77. That points to moderate covalency.\n- 10Dq = 4 070 cm⁻¹ is typical of the weak splitting found in tetrahedral complexes.\n- The second Racah parameter, C, can't be found from these bands. All three are quartet-to-quartet transitions, and their energies don't depend on C.\n\n{\"Dq\": 407, \"B\": 748, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Td d7 bands fit by d3 analogue: 10Dq = ν1, ν2+ν3 = 15B+30Dq; C not determinable from quartet bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7974,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m